How the Program Works

1. Start an Activity

The user enters an activity and their name.

The program records:

The user's name.

The activity description.

The start time.

The end time, initially set to None.

The activity information is stored in a dictionary and added to the sessions list.

2. Stop an Activity

The user enters their name.

The program searches the recorded sessions for an activity belonging to that user that has not yet been stopped.

When a matching activity is found, the program records the end time and calculates the duration.

3. Calculate Time Spent

The program subtracts the start time from the end time.

The difference is stored as a timedelta object representing the time spent on the activity.

4. View Daily Activity

The daily activity function examines the recorded sessions.

It skips activities that have not been completed and calculates the time spent on completed activities.

The program is intended to summarise the total time spent on each activity.

5. Main Menu

The application provides a menu with four options:

Enter an activity and start tracking.

Enter a name to stop an activity.

View the activity summary.

Quit the application.

The menu uses a while loop to allow users to perform multiple actions until they choose to quit.
